contrib/pg_bp_osic/README

pg_bp_osic -- One-Stage Insert Clock (OSIC)
============================================

pg_bp_osic provides the OSIC buffer replacement algorithm as a PostgreSQL
extension.  OSIC is inspired by the LeanStore buffer manager and is
designed for high-concurrency, many-core systems where the buffer hit
path must be as cheap as possible.  OSIC can be used with dynamic buffer
pools:

    CREATE EXTENSION pg_bp_osic;
    CREATE BUFFER POOL my_osic_pool HANDLER osic_pool_handler SIZE '33554432';
    CREATE TABLE hot_data (...) WITH (buffer_pool = 'my_osic_pool');

Algorithm
=========

OSIC uses a boolean "cool" flag per buffer rather than a usage counter
or linked list.  The cache hit path is completely lock-free: it performs
a single atomic AND to clear the cool flag.  No spinlock is acquired,
no list is walked, and no counter is incremented on the critical path.

Eviction is a two-stage clock sweep:

    Stage 1: If the buffer is "hot" (cool flag clear), mark it "cool"
             (set the flag) and advance the clock hand.
    Stage 2: If the buffer is "cool" (flag set) and unpinned, evict it.

A buffer must therefore survive at least one full sweep of the clock
hand before it can be evicted, providing basic scan resistance.

Partitioned Clock
-----------------

The buffer range is divided into up to 16 partitions, each with its own
clock hand protected by its own spinlock.  The partition count scales
with the pool size:

    Pool size          Partitions
    ---------          ----------
    < 256 buffers      1
    256 - 1023         2
    1024 - 4095        4
    4096 - 16383       8
    >= 16384           16

Backends choose a starting partition based on their process number, so
different backends can sweep different partitions concurrently.  The
partition spinlock is held only during the clock sweep; the hot-path
on_hit callback never acquires a spinlock.

When to Use OSIC
=================

OSIC is a good fit for:

  - Many-core systems (32+ cores) where spinlock contention on the hit
    path is the bottleneck
  - Workloads with very high hit rates, where eviction is rare and the
    cost of on_hit dominates
  - Simple, predictable workloads (e.g., a working set that fits in
    cache most of the time)

Consider ARC or CAR instead if:

  - The workload has frequent hot/cold transitions that benefit from
    adaptive ghost-list tuning
  - You need VACUUM-aware or prefetch-aware buffer management
  - The working set regularly exceeds the cache size, making eviction
    quality more important than hit-path speed

Statistics
==========

Install the extension to get the pg_stat_osic view:

    SELECT * FROM pg_stat_osic;

Columns:

    pool_name       -- name of the buffer pool
    pool_oid        -- OID of the pool (NULL for built-in pools)
    nbuffers        -- total buffers in this pool
    hot_count       -- buffers currently in "hot" state (cool flag clear)
    cool_count      -- buffers currently in "cool" state (cool flag set)
    hits            -- total cache hits (aggregated across all partitions)
    misses          -- total cache misses
    evictions       -- total evictions
    cooling_sweeps  -- total stage-1 cooling sweeps (hot -> cool transitions)

Per-partition statistics are maintained with per-backend local counters
that flush to shared atomic counters every 256 increments, reducing
cross-core cache-line bouncing.

Limitations
===========

No adaptation: OSIC has no ghost lists and no frequency tracking.  It
cannot learn from workload changes the way ARC or CAR do.

Binary granularity: The cool/hot flag is coarser than the usage_count
(0-5) used by the built-in PostgreSQL clock sweep, or the explicit list
ordering provided by ARC.  This may cause premature eviction of pages
that would survive longer under finer-grained algorithms.

No VACUUM integration: OSIC does not implement the hint_vacuum callback.
VACUUM-loaded pages enter the cache as "hot" and must survive a full
clock cycle before eviction, which may pollute the cache during large
VACUUM operations.

No prefetch integration: OSIC does not implement the prefetch_hint
callback.

Partition 0 miss attribution: Cache miss statistics are always attributed
to partition 0 because there is no buffer context at the time of a miss.
This is a minor reporting imprecision.

Reference
=========

V. Leis et al., "LeanStore: In-Memory Data Management Beyond Main
Memory", Proceedings of IEEE ICDE 2018.
https://db.in.tum.de/~leis/papers/leanstore.pdf
